---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CATEGORIES, SITE } from '../../lib/constants';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  const blogPosts = [
    {
      title: 'How to Choose the Right Smartphone in 2026',
      slug: 'how-to-choose-the-right-smartphone-in-2026',
      date: '2026-07-15',
      category: 'tech',
      readTime: '12 min read',
    },
    {
      title: 'Top 10 Laptop Comparisons That Actually Matter in 2026',
      slug: 'top-10-laptop-comparisons-that-actually-matter',
      date: '2026-07-22',
      category: 'tech',
      readTime: '15 min read',
    },
    {
      title: 'Cryptocurrency Comparison: What to Look For',
      slug: 'cryptocurrency-comparison-what-to-look-for',
      date: '2026-07-28',
      category: 'crypto',
      readTime: '10 min read',
    },
    {
      title: 'Travel Destination Comparison: Europe vs Asia',
      slug: 'travel-destination-comparison-europe-vs-asia',
      date: '2026-08-02',
      category: 'travel',
      readTime: '14 min read',
    },
    {
      title: 'Electric vs Gas Cars: The Complete Comparison',
      slug: 'electric-vs-gas-cars-complete-comparison',
      date: '2026-08-05',
      category: 'cars',
      readTime: '18 min read',
    },
    {
      title: 'Understanding AI Model Comparisons: GPT vs Claude vs Gemini',
      slug: 'understanding-ai-model-comparisons-gpt-vs-claude-vs-gemini',
      date: '2026-08-07',
      category: 'ai-models',
      readTime: '11 min read',
    },
  ];
  return blogPosts.map((post) => ({
    params: { slug: post.slug },
    props: { post },
  }));
}

const { post } = Astro.props;

const cat = CATEGORIES.find(c => c.slug === post.category);

const CONTENT: Record<string, string> = {
  'how-to-choose-the-right-smartphone-in-2026': `
    <p>The smartphone market in 2026 is more fragmented than ever. With Apple, Samsung, Google, OnePlus, and Xiaomi all releasing multiple flagship models per year, choosing the right phone requires a systematic approach. Gone are the days when you could simply pick between an iPhone and an Android device — today's decisions involve camera systems, AI features, ecosystem lock-in, and long-term software support.</p>

    <h2>Step 1: Define Your Budget Range</h2>
    <p>Smartphones in 2026 range from $200 budget models to $2,000+ foldable flagships. Before falling in love with a particular model, set a realistic budget. The sweet spot for most users is $600–$900, where you get flagship-grade performance without paying for marginal improvements. If you keep your phone for three or more years, investing in a higher-tier model often makes financial sense due to longer software support.</p>

    <h2>Step 2: Prioritize What Matters to You</h2>
    <p>Ask yourself honestly: what do you actually use your phone for? Photographers should prioritize camera sensor size, optical zoom range, and computational photography capabilities. Gamers need high-refresh-rate displays, vapor chamber cooling, and sustained performance. Business users benefit from desktop modes, stylus support, and productivity app ecosystems. There is no universally "best" phone — only the best phone for your specific needs.</p>

    <h2>Step 3: Evaluate the Camera System</h2>
    <p>Camera marketing is misleading. A 200MP sensor means nothing if the lens quality is poor or the image processing is aggressive. Look for phones with larger physical sensor sizes (measured in fractions of an inch, like 1/1.3"), optical image stabilization on multiple lenses, and natural color science. The iPhone 16 Pro and Pixel 9 Pro lead in computational photography, while Samsung's Galaxy S25 Ultra offers the most versatile zoom range at 10x optical.</p>

    <h2>Step 4: Consider Software Longevity</h2>
    <p>Apple now promises 7 years of iOS updates for its latest devices. Google offers 7 years for Pixel phones. Samsung provides 6 years for its Galaxy S series. Budget Android phones from other manufacturers may only receive 2 years of updates. If you plan to keep your phone long-term, software support directly impacts both security and feature availability.</p>

    <h2>Step 5: Test the Ecosystem</h2>
    <p>Your phone does not exist in isolation. If you own a Mac, iPad, or Apple Watch, an iPhone integrates seamlessly through AirDrop, Handoff, and Universal Clipboard. Galaxy phones pair perfectly with Galaxy Buds and Galaxy Watch. Pixel phones offer the cleanest Google services integration. Switching ecosystems later means repurchasing accessories and learning new workflows.</p>

    <h2>Step 6: Compare Real-World Performance</h2>
    <p>Benchmarks tell only part of the story. A phone that scores highest on synthetic tests may throttle quickly under sustained load. Look for reviews that test real-world performance: app launch speed, gaming frame rate consistency, and multitasking behavior. Battery life tests should simulate your actual usage pattern, not just video playback loops.</p>

    <h2>The Verdict</h2>
    <p>Choosing the right smartphone in 2026 is ultimately about matching the device to your specific needs and habits. Use the framework above to narrow your options, then read our detailed head-to-head comparisons for the final decision. The best phone is the one that disappears into your daily life — reliable, familiar, and capable when you need it.</p>
  `,
  'top-10-laptop-comparisons-that-actually-matter': `
    <p>The laptop market in 2026 is saturated with options. Every manufacturer releases dozens of models each year, and tech media churns out comparison articles by the hundreds. But most of these comparisons test identical specs at identical price points — they are noise. We have identified the 10 laptop matchups that will actually help you make a purchasing decision in 2026.</p>

    <h2>1. MacBook Pro M4 vs Dell XPS 16 (2026)</h2>
    <p>This is the flagship showdown. The MacBook Pro M4 delivers industry-leading battery life (up to 22 hours) and silent operation, while the Dell XPS 16 offers a stunning OLED touchscreen, better port selection, and Windows-specific features like DirectSR for gaming. For creative professionals embedded in the Apple ecosystem, the MacBook Pro remains unmatched. For Windows power users who need maximum flexibility, the XPS 16 is compelling.</p>

    <h2>2. ThinkPad X1 Carbon Gen 14 vs HP EliteBook 1040 G12</h2>
    <p>For business travelers, this comparison is everything. The ThinkPad X1 Carbon retains its legendary keyboard and MIL-STD durability, while the HP EliteBook 1040 offers a brighter display and superior conference call audio. Both offer vPro processors and enterprise security features. The winner depends on whether you prioritize typing experience or presentation capabilities.</p>

    <h2>3. MacBook Air M4 vs Surface Laptop 8</h2>
    <p>The mainstream ultrabook battle. MacBook Air M4 starts at $999 with no fan, no noise, and 15-hour battery life. Surface Laptop 8 starts at $1,099 with a haptic touchpad, higher-resolution display, and Copilot+ AI features. Windows users who want a clean, premium experience should consider the Surface; everyone else benefits from Apple Silicon's efficiency.</p>

    <h2>4. Razer Blade 16 (2026) vs ASUS ROG Zephyrus G16</h2>
    <p>Gaming laptops have evolved beyond raw specs. The Razer Blade 16 offers CNC aluminum build quality and dual-mode mini-LED display, while the ASUS ROG Zephyrus G16 is lighter, offers better battery life for non-gaming use, and includes an AMD Ryzen AI 9 HX processor. Razer wins on prestige; ASUS wins on versatility.</p>

    <h2>5. Lenovo Yoga Pro 9i vs Samsung Galaxy Book5 Pro 360</h2>
    <p>The 2-in-1 convertible category. The Yoga Pro 9i features a gorgeous 3K display and excellent hinge mechanism, while the Galaxy Book5 Pro 360 includes an S Pen and seamless Samsung ecosystem integration. Galaxy phone owners should choose Samsung; everyone else benefits from Lenovo's superior keyboard and build quality.</p>

    <h2>6. Framework Laptop 16 vs HP OmniBook X</h2>
    <p>The modular vs AI-first battle. Framework's Laptop 16 lets you upgrade every component, from the GPU module to the keyboard layout. The HP OmniBook X is a sealed Snapdragon X Elite machine with 26-hour battery life and built-in AI acceleration. Tinkerers should choose Framework; users who want a reliable appliance should pick the OmniBook.</p>

    <h2>7. Acer Swift Go 14 vs ASUS Vivobook S 15</h2>
    <p>The budget ultrabook comparison that matters. Both sit around $700–$850, but the Swift Go offers a better display and lighter chassis, while the Vivobook provides more ports and a larger screen. For students and budget-conscious buyers, either is fine, but the Swift Go edges ahead on portability.</p>

    <h2>8. MSI Titan 18 HX vs Alienware Area-51</h2>
    <p>Desktop replacement territory. The MSI Titan features liquid metal cooling and desktop-class RTX 5090 performance. The Alienware Area-51 offers a more modern design and better thermal acoustics. Both are massive and expensive, but the Titan delivers slightly higher raw performance for creators who need maximum throughput.</p>

    <h2>9. LG Gram Pro 17 vs Samsung Galaxy Book4 Ultra</h2>
    <p>The ultra-light large display category. LG Gram Pro 17 weighs just 3.08 lbs despite its 17-inch screen, making it ideal for mobile professionals. The Galaxy Book4 Ultra packs more GPU power in a 16-inch form factor. Choose LG for portability; choose Samsung for creative workloads.</p>

    <h2>10. Chromebook Plus 515 vs MacBook Air M4</h2>
    <p>The education and light productivity showdown. Chromebook Plus models now offer 12-hour battery life, 16GB RAM options, and Linux support for around $600. The MacBook Air M4 remains the gold standard at $999, but for students and casual users, the Chromebook Plus offers genuine value. The gap has never been smaller.</p>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
    <p>The best laptop comparison is the one that matches your specific workflow and budget. Use these 10 matchups as a starting point, then explore our detailed side-by-side breakdowns for specs, benchmarks, and real-world usage tests.</p>
  `,
  'cryptocurrency-comparison-what-to-look-for': `
    <p>Cryptocurrency comparison is fundamentally different from comparing traditional assets. You are not just evaluating price performance — you are assessing technology, community, governance, regulatory risk, and long-term viability. Whether you are comparing Bitcoin to Ethereum or evaluating the latest DePIN token, this framework will help you make informed decisions.</p>

    <h2>1. Understand the Token's Purpose</h2>
    <p>Every cryptocurrency should solve a real problem. Bitcoin was created as decentralized digital gold — a store of value outside government control. Ethereum introduced programmable smart contracts. Chainlink provides decentralized oracle services. When comparing cryptocurrencies, start by understanding what problem each token solves and whether that problem actually needs a blockchain solution.</p>

    <h2>2. Evaluate Tokenomics</h2>
    <p>Tokenomics refers to how a token's supply, distribution, and incentives work. Key metrics include: total supply vs circulating supply, inflation rate, allocation to team and investors (and their vesting schedules), and whether the token has real utility beyond speculation. A token with unlimited supply and high inflation will struggle to appreciate regardless of its technology.</p>

    <h2>3. Analyze the Consensus Mechanism</h2>
    <p>Proof of Work (Bitcoin) provides the strongest security guarantees but consumes enormous energy. Proof of Stake (Ethereum post-Merge) is more energy-efficient but introduces different centralization risks. Delegated Proof of Stake (Solana, Cardano variants) sacrifices decentralization for speed. Each mechanism has trade-offs, and understanding them helps you assess long-term viability.</p>

    <h2>4. Assess Developer Activity</h2>
    <p>A blockchain without active developers is a ghost town. Check GitHub repositories, developer documentation quality, hackathon participation, and ecosystem grants programs. Ethereum's dominance is not just due to first-mover advantage — it has the largest developer community in crypto. Compare monthly active developers, not just total historical commits.</p>

    <h2>5. Examine Real-World Adoption</h2>
    <p>Speculative price appreciation means nothing without usage. Look for: daily active addresses, transaction volume (excluding wash trading), total value locked (for DeFi protocols), and enterprise partnerships. A cryptocurrency with 100,000 daily users is fundamentally different from one with 10 million, regardless of market cap.</p>

    <h2>6. Consider Regulatory Risk</h2>
    <p>Regulatory clarity varies dramatically by jurisdiction. The SEC's stance on token classification, MiCA in Europe, and China's crypto ban all impact token value. When compare investments, consider which tokens have the clearest regulatory standing and which face existential regulatory threats. Bitcoin and Ether benefit from the most regulatory clarity.</p>

    <h2>7. Liquidity and Market Depth</h2>
    <p>A token listed on major exchanges with deep order books is fundamentally safer than one traded only on decentralized exchanges. Check: daily trading volume, number of exchange listings, bid-ask spreads, and slippage on large trades. Low liquidity means you may not be able to exit positions during market stress.</p>

    <h2>Comparing Specific Categories</h2>
    <p>When comparing within categories, focus on category-specific metrics. For Layer 1 chains, compare TPS, time to finality, and developer tooling. For DeFi protocols, compare TVL, fee revenue, and security audit history. For governance tokens, compare voter participation rates and treasury management. Generic price comparisons miss the substance.</p>

    <h2>The Comparison Framework in Practice</h2>
    <p>Apply this framework systematically: start with purpose, then tokenomics, then technical architecture, then ecosystem metrics, then regulatory positioning. Score each factor objectively. The token with the highest composite score — not the highest recent price gain — is typically the stronger long-term hold.</p>

    <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
    <p>Cryptocurrency markets are driven by narratives and hype, but sustainable value comes from real utility and sound economics. Use comparison as a tool for understanding, not just picking winners. The best investment thesis is one you can defend with data when the market is down 80%.</p>
  `,
  'travel-destination-comparison-europe-vs-asia': `
    <p>Choosing between Europe and Asia for travel, relocation, or retirement is one of the most impactful lifestyle decisions you can make. Both continents offer extraordinary diversity, rich culture, and world-class experiences — but they differ dramatically in cost, pace of life, infrastructure, and cultural approach. This comparison draws from extensive travel data, expat surveys, and cost-of-living indices to help you decide.</p>

    <h2>Cost of Living</h2>
    <p>Asia wins decisively on cost. In Southeast Asia (Thailand, Vietnam, Indonesia, Malaysia), a comfortable expatriate lifestyle costs $1,500–$2,500 per month including rent in a modern apartment, dining out, and domestic help. In Western Europe (France, Germany, Netherlands), the same lifestyle costs $3,500–$5,500 per month. Eastern Europe and Southern Europe (Portugal, Greece, Czech Republic) offer middle-ground options at $2,000–$3,000 per month.</p>

    <h2>Safety and Healthcare</h2>
    <p>Western Europe maintains the highest safety standards globally, with low violent crime rates and comprehensive public healthcare systems. Japan, South Korea, Singapore, and Taiwan match European safety standards. Southeast Asia is generally safe but requires more vigilance regarding traffic, petty crime, and health precautions. Healthcare quality varies widely: Singapore and Japan offer world-class care, while rural Southeast Asia may lack advanced facilities. Medical tourism from Europe to Asia (Thailand, Malaysia) is common precisely because Asian hospitals offer European-quality care at a fraction of the cost.</p>

    <h2>Culture and Lifestyle</h2>
    <p>Europe offers historical depth — thousands of years of continuous civilization visible in architecture, museums, and urban fabric. The café culture, public spaces, and work-life balance (especially in Northern Europe) create a particular rhythm of life. Asia offers cultural intensity — the sensory richness of street food, night markets, temples, and festivals creates an entirely different experience. The pace of life varies: Japan is structured and punctual; Thailand is relaxed and flexible; European cities fall somewhere in between.</p>

    <h2>Language and Communication</h2>
    <p>English proficiency varies significantly. In the Netherlands, Scandinavia, and Ireland, English is nearly universal in professional settings. In Southern and Eastern Europe, English is common in tourist areas but less prevalent daily. In Asia, Singapore and the Philippines are English-speaking; Japan and South Korea have limited English proficiency; Thailand's tourist zones accommodate English but expats eventually need basic Thai for daily life.</p>

    <h2>Infrastructure and Transportation</h2>
    <p>Europe's train network is legendary. The Eurail system connects major cities efficiently, and budget airlines make even remote destinations accessible. High-speed rail in France, Spain, and Germany rivals air travel for city-center to city-center journeys. Asia's infrastructure is rapidly modernizing: Japan's Shinkansen, China's high-speed rail network, and Singapore's MRT are world-class. However, intercity travel in Southeast Asia often involves buses or budget flights with less consistency.</p>

    <h2>Food Culture</h2>
    <p>Both continents are food paradises, but differently. European cuisine emphasizes technique, tradition, and ingredient quality. A meal in Italy, France, or Spain is a deliberate experience. Asian cuisine emphasizes balance, bold flavors, and variety. A single street food meal in Bangkok or Penang can be a culinary revelation. For daily eating, Asia's street food culture means excellent meals cost $2–$5, while European casual dining starts at $15–$25.</p>

    <h2>Climate</h2>
    <p>Europe offers four distinct seasons, with Mediterranean warmth in summer and continental cold in Northern Europe in winter. Asia spans tropical (year-round warmth in Southeast Asia), subtropical (four seasons in Japan and Korea), and arid zones (Central Asia). Climate preference is deeply personal, but those seeking year-round warmth should focus on Southeast Asia or Southern Europe.</p>

    <h2>Visa and Residency</h2>
    <p>Both continents have introduced digital nomad and remote work visas in 2024–2026. Europe's offerings include Portugal's D8 visa, Spain's digital nomad visa, and Greece's remote work permit. Asia offers Thailand's Long-Term Resident visa, Malaysia's DE Rantara program, and Japan's specified activities visa. Income requirements, tax implications, and path-to-permanence vary significantly between countries and should be researched carefully.</p>

    <h2>The Verdict</h2>
    <p>Neither continent is objectively better — they serve different life stages and preferences. Asia excels for budget-conscious travelers, food lovers, and those seeking tropical climates. Europe excels for history enthusiasts, public transit users, and those prioritizing healthcare and safety. Many experienced expats split their time: summers in Europe for culture, winters in Asia for warmth and value. The best choice aligns with your budget, climate preferences, and lifestyle priorities.</p>
  `,
  'electric-vs-gas-cars-complete-comparison': `
    <p>The transition from gas to electric vehicles is no longer a question of if but when. By 2026, EVs have matured significantly — charging infrastructure has expanded, battery costs have plummeted, and electric drivetrains have proven their reliability. However, gas cars still hold genuine advantages in specific scenarios. This comprehensive comparison covers every factor that matters for your purchase decision.</p>

    <h2>Purchase Price</h2>
    <p>The EV price premium has narrowed considerably. A Tesla Model 3 starts at $39,990, while a comparable Honda Accord starts at $28,000. Federal tax credits (up to $7,500 in the US) can close this gap further. However, when you factor in lower operating costs, the total cost of ownership often favors EVs within 3–4 years. Luxury EVs still carry a premium over comparable gas vehicles, though this gap is closing as manufacturers achieve economies of scale.</p>

    <h2>Fuel and Energy Costs</h2>
    <p>Electricity is significantly cheaper than gasoline per mile. Charging an EV at home costs approximately $0.04–$0.06 per mile (based on average US electricity rates of $0.14/kWh). Gasoline at $3.50/gallon with a 35 MPG vehicle costs $0.10 per mile. DC fast charging is more expensive at $0.25–$0.40/kWh but still competitive with premium gasoline. Over 15,000 miles annually, EV owners save $600–$1,200 on fuel costs alone.</p>

    <h2>Maintenance and Reliability</h2>
    <p>EVs have far fewer moving parts — no engine oil, transmission fluid, spark plugs, timing belts, or exhaust systems. Brake wear is dramatically reduced thanks to regenerative braking. Tire wear can be slightly higher due to instant torque and heavier vehicle weight. Overall, EV maintenance costs average 40% less than comparable gas vehicles. The primary long-term concern is battery degradation, but modern batteries retain 80–90% capacity after 200,000 miles.</p>

    <h2>Range and Refueling</h2>
    <p>Range anxiety is largely a thing of the past for daily use. Most 2026 EVs offer 250–350 miles of real-world range, sufficient for 99% of daily driving. The gap appears on road trips: a gas car refuels in 5 minutes, while even the fastest EV chargers require 15–25 minutes for an 80% charge. If you frequently drive 400+ mile days in areas with sparse charging infrastructure, a gas or hybrid vehicle remains more practical.</p>

    <h2>Performance and Driving Experience</h2>
    <p>EVs deliver instant torque, silent operation, and low center of gravity (battery pack mounted under the floor). Acceleration from standstill is effortless — even mid-range EVs outperform most gas sports cars in 0–60 mph times. The smooth, vibration-free ride is noticeable immediately. However, driving enthusiasts miss the mechanical engagement of manual transmissions, engine sound feedback, and the weight distribution of longitudinally-mounted gas engines.</p>

    <h2>Environmental Impact</h2>
    <p>EVs produce zero tailpipe emissions, but their overall environmental footprint depends on the local electricity grid. In regions with heavy coal dependence, an efficient hybrid may have lower lifetime CO2 than an EV charged on the grid. However, as grids worldwide add renewable energy, EVs become cleaner over their lifetime while gas cars do not. Manufacturing emissions for EVs are higher due to battery production, but this carbon debt is typically repaid within 1–2 years of driving.</p>

    <h2>Charging Infrastructure</h2>
    <p>Public charging has improved dramatically. Tesla's Supercharger network is now open to other brands via NACS adapters. Electrified America, ChargePoint, and regional networks offer reliable fast charging along major highways. The remaining challenge is rural areas and apartment buildings without dedicated parking. Home charging remains the most convenient option — a Level 2 charger (240V) adds 25–35 miles of range per hour.</p>

    <h2>Resale Value</h2>
    <p>EV resale values have stabilized after an initial dip. Tesla vehicles retain value better than most EVs due to software updates, Supercharger access, and brand strength. Some EV models from legacy manufacturers depreciate faster than their gas equivalents. Battery health reports and remaining warranty coverage increasingly influence resale values. Certified pre-owned EVs with verified battery health reports are becoming a strong value proposition.</p>

    <h2>Who Should Buy an EV?</h2>
    <p>Buy an EV if you: have home charging access, drive under 100 miles daily, value low maintenance costs, and want the quietest, smoothest daily driving experience. Consider a gas or hybrid if you: frequently tow heavy loads, drive in remote areas with no charging infrastructure, park on the street without outlet access, or plan to keep the vehicle 15+ years without battery replacement.</p>

    <h2>Who Should Stick with Gas?</h2>
    <p>Gas vehicles remain the better choice for rural drivers, frequent long-distance haulers, performance driving enthusiasts who value mechanical engagement, and budget buyers looking for the lowest possible purchase price. Hybrids offer a compelling middle ground — 50+ MPG with zero range anxiety and no charging requirement. The optimal choice depends entirely on your specific usage pattern, not ideology.</p>
  `,
  'understanding-ai-model-comparisons-gpt-vs-claude-vs-gemini': `
    <p>The AI model landscape in 2026 is more competitive than ever. OpenAI's GPT-5, Anthropic's Claude 4, Google's Gemini 3, Meta's Llama 4, and numerous specialized models compete for developers and enterprise customers. Comparing AI models requires understanding that different models optimize for different objectives — there is no single "best" model, only the best model for your specific use case.</p>

    <h2>Understanding Benchmarks vs Reality</h2>
    <p>Public benchmarks (MMLU, HumanEval, GPQA) provide useful directional data but do not predict real-world performance for your specific application. A model that scores highest on coding benchmarks may underperform on creative writing. A model that excels at reasoning tasks may be slower and more expensive for simple classification work. Always evaluate models using your own test cases before committing to production deployment.</p>

    <h2>GPT-5: The Generalist Powerhouse</h2>
    <p>OpenAI's GPT-5 leads in raw capability breadth. It handles code generation, mathematical reasoning, creative writing, and multimodal analysis at the highest level. The model's strength is its consistency across diverse tasks — it rarely fails completely on any given input. Weaknesses include higher pricing ($3–$15 per 1M tokens depending on variant), occasional overconfidence in incorrect answers, and slower response times on complex reasoning tasks. Best for: general-purpose applications, code assistance, and situations where you need reliable performance across many task types.</p>

    <h2>Claude 4: The Safety and Nuance Champion</h2>
    <p>Anthropic's Claude 4 models (Sonnet, Opus) emphasize helpfulness, harmlessness, and honesty. Claude excels at nuanced writing, long-form analysis, and tasks requiring careful reasoning about ethics, policy, or sensitive topics. The 200K context window (with experimental 500K support) allows analysis of entire codebases or lengthy documents. Weaknesses include slightly lower raw performance on competitive math benchmarks and more conservative responses that may refuse borderline requests. Best for: research analysis, content creation, legal and policy work, and applications where safety is paramount.</p>

    <h2>Gemini 3 Pro: The Multimodal Native</h2>
    <p>Google's Gemini 3 Pro was designed from the ground up for multimodal processing — it handles text, images, audio, and video as native input types rather than bolt-on capabilities. This architectural advantage shows in tasks requiring cross-modal reasoning: analyzing a video clip and generating a written summary, or interpreting a diagram and answering technical questions. Weaknesses include less mature developer tooling compared to OpenAI and occasionally verbose responses. Best for: media analysis, education applications, and multimodal content creation.</p>

    <h2>Llama 4: The Open-Source Alternative</h2>
    <p>Meta's Llama 4 offers near-frontier performance with the freedom to self-host, fine-tune, and deploy without API dependencies. For organizations with data sovereignty requirements, regulatory constraints, or high-volume usage that makes API costs prohibitive, Llama 4 is increasingly viable. The gap between open-source and closed-source models has narrowed significantly — Llama 4 matches GPT-4-level performance on most benchmarks. Weaknesses include higher infrastructure requirements for self-hosting and less frequent updates. Best for: enterprises with privacy requirements, high-volume applications, and research teams.</p>

    <h2>Pricing and Total Cost of Ownership</h2>
    <p>API pricing varies dramatically. GPT-5 Turbo: $3/1M input, $15/1M output tokens. Claude 4 Sonnet: $3/1M input, $15/1M output. Gemini 3 Pro: $2.50/1M input, $10/1M output. Self-hosting Llama 4 on cloud GPUs costs $5–$15/hour for A100-class hardware. For applications processing billions of tokens monthly, self-hosting becomes cost-effective. For variable workloads, API pricing offers better flexibility. Always model your actual token usage before choosing.</p>

    <h2>Latency and Throughput</h2>
    <p>Response speed varies significantly by model and provider. GPT-5 typically returns first tokens in 200–500ms. Claude 4 is slightly slower at 300–700ms. Gemini 3 Pro is fastest at 150–400ms for standard queries. For applications requiring sub-100ms responses, consider smaller distilled models (GPT-5 Mini, Claude 4 Haiku) that sacrifice minimal capability for significant speed improvements.</p>

    <h2>Choosing the Right Model for Your Use Case</h2>
    <p>Code generation: GPT-5 or Claude 4 Sonnet. Long-form writing: Claude 4 Opus. Multimodal analysis: Gemini 3 Pro. Cost-sensitive high-volume: Llama 4 self-hosted. Real-time applications: Claude 4 Haiku or GPT-5 Mini. Enterprise with compliance needs: Llama 4 private deployment or Claude 4 via AWS Bedrock with HIPAA/SOC2 compliance.</p>

    <h2>The Future of AI Model Comparison</h2>
    <p>The AI model market is converging on a few clear leaders but diverging in specialization. Rather than one model winning everything, the future is model routing — automatically selecting the optimal model for each query based on complexity, modality, latency requirements, and cost constraints. Platforms like OpenRouter and Helicone already offer this capability. The most sophisticated AI applications in 2026 will use multiple models intelligently rather than betting everything on a single provider.</p>
  `,
};

const articleContent = CONTENT[post.slug] || '';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${post.title} - ChooseVS`}
  description={post.title}
>
  <article class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <!-- Article Header -->
        <header class="mb-10">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="text-2xl">{cat?.icon}</span>
            <span class="badge-primary">{cat?.name}</span>
            <span class="text-sm text-surface-500">{post.date}</span>
            <span class="text-sm text-surface-500">{post.readTime}</span>
          </div>
          <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-surface-900 mb-4">
            {post.title}
          </h1>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-surface-600">
            <span>By ChooseVS Team</span>
            <span>|</span>
            <span>Last updated: {post.date}</span>
          </div>
        </header>

        <!-- Article Body -->
        <div class="prose-custom" set:html={articleContent} />

        <!-- Back to Blog -->
        <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-surface-200">
          <a href="/blog" class="inline-flex items-center text-primary-600 hover:text-primary-700 font-semibold">
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7" />
            </svg>
            Back to All Articles
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Schema.org Article Markup -->
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": post.title,
    "author": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": SITE.author,
    },
    "publisher": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": SITE.name,
      "logo": {
        "@type": "ImageObject",
        "url": `${SITE.url}/favicon.svg`,
      },
    },
    "datePublished": post.date,
    "dateModified": post.date,
    "mainEntityOfPage": {
      "@type": "WebPage",
      "@id": `${SITE.url}/blog/${post.slug}`,
    },
  })} />
</BaseLayout>
